ClonoLoom's host, interface shell, installers and workflows are adapted from its sibling programs
SituCraft, MaveScape, CytoWeave and Proteoscope (https://github.com/robert-mcdermott), by the same
author under the same license.

The repository-only agent benchmark follows CytoWeave's Apache-2.0 task/grader and control
pattern, using ClonoLoom's own fixtures, permission policy and native scientific evidence.

The HDF5 metadata checksum helper and export writer are adapted from SituCraft's HDF5 writer
under the same Apache-2.0 license. The writer is extended for AnnData data types and encodings.
They implement Bob Jenkins' public-domain lookup3/hashlittle checksum.

The flat Parquet writer and compact-protocol emission are adapted from SituCraft v0.9.0
under the same Apache-2.0 license, extended for nullable fields and bounded scientific exports.

The Excel workbook presentation follows CytoWeave's Apache-2.0 header, width and frozen-row
pattern. ClonoLoom implements its own native Go writer and exact JSON evidence mapping.

The beta and Student t distribution helpers in internal/stats are adapted from SituCraft's
Apache-2.0 statistics helpers by the same author.

This product includes data derived from:
- the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee's gene names (https://www.genenames.org; CC0): the names of
  the human T-cell receptor and immunoglobulin genes (internal/genes/human.go);
- the GRCh38 human genome at Ensembl's annotation (https://www.ensembl.org; Ensembl imposes no
  restrictions on the use of its data): the sequences of the human TRB gene segments the simulator
  recombines (internal/simulate/germline.go);
- 10x Genomics' "10k Human PBMCs, 5' v2.0, Chromium Controller" dataset
  (https://www.10xgenomics.com/datasets; CC BY 4.0): the TRB V and J gene usage and junction
  lengths the simulator follows (internal/simulate/germline.go).

Public datasets, other reference germline sets, recombination models and specificity databases are
not part of this product. When a user asks, ClonoLoom downloads them from their publishers under
their own licenses and terms, which it shows before each download and records with every result
that uses them.

The ADC metadata client follows the AIRR Community's ADC 1.2.0 protocol specification in
AIRR Standards 1.5.1 (CC BY 4.0), https://docs.airr-community.org/en/v1.5.1/api/adc_api.html.
Its destination list is the AIRR Community's approved ADC registry at revision
4ae159f2799594a2c73669527f6fba97fc8995d7 (https://github.com/airr-community/adc-registry).
The client is an independent implementation; no repository server implementation is included.
